WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.706
Jarvis is a personal Android assistant that combines chat with observations from your phone.

00:00:05.706 --> 00:00:13.857
It can manage notes and reminders, explain sampled activity history, work with Google Calendar, and retrieve information from Drive files.

00:00:13.857 --> 00:00:22.823
Think of it as a phone application connected to a modular Python backend, with a separate embedding service and an indexing worker.

00:00:22.823 --> 00:00:25.676
This explanation follows the current local code.

00:00:25.676 --> 00:00:30.159
It does not verify which revision is running in the cloud.

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:33.604
The visible interface is built with Flutter.

00:00:33.604 --> 00:00:38.526
Its main tabs are Chat, Activity, Reminders, Notes, and Settings.

00:00:38.526 --> 00:00:44.924
Singleton services and Flutter notifiers manage transport, local data, sensors and Google connections.

00:00:44.924 --> 00:00:56.736
Underneath, Kotlin handles Android capabilities that need to work when Flutter is closed: activity recognition, background location, upload workers, push messages and reminder alarms.

00:00:56.736 --> 00:00:59.197
Method channels connect these layers.

00:00:59.197 --> 00:01:03.134
The frontend is therefore more than the screens.

00:01:03.134 --> 00:01:08.056
Native Android code is an essential part of its reliability.

00:01:08.056 --> 00:01:12.885
The phone stores app records in a local SQLite database.

00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:16.266
Flutter telemetry also has a SQLite outbox.

00:01:16.266 --> 00:01:22.544
Native background events use a different queue: a JSON array committed to SharedPreferences.

00:01:22.544 --> 00:01:28.339
This differs from the README, which describes that native queue as SQLite.

00:01:28.339 --> 00:01:33.652
WorkManager uploads queued events and retries failures while preserving their identifiers.

00:01:33.652 --> 00:01:37.998
Explicit sensor recordings produce CSV files and JSON summaries.

00:01:37.998 --> 00:01:45.242
These recordings are distinct from the journey sessions and activity intervals reconstructed by the backend.

00:01:45.242 --> 00:01:51.037
Offline capture is supported, but cloud chat and integrations still need connectivity.

00:01:51.037 --> 00:01:58.942
The backend uses FastAPI to expose telemetry, commands, reminders, notes, synchronization, recording backups and file operations.

00:01:58.942 --> 00:02:04.870
Chat progress uses server sent events, with request status and reconnect support.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.834
This is not a WebSocket design.

00:02:07.834 --> 00:02:13.269
The API invokes a cached LangGraph workflow and deterministic domain services.

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:21.173
Tier one and tier two are reasoning paths inside the same application, not independent deployed microservices.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:28.090
Blocking graph and cloud operations are moved to worker threads in important async endpoints.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:34.019
A separate embedding service and an indexing job handle file retrieval workloads.

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:38.261
Every graph run begins with normalization and context loading.

00:02:38.261 --> 00:02:41.560
Context events go through an evidence gate.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:51.458
Ordinary sparse observations can use the deterministic reducer directly, while ambiguous feature bearing telemetry can invoke tier one and its tools.

00:02:51.458 --> 00:02:53.815
Commands go through intent routing.

00:02:53.815 --> 00:03:00.414
Greetings have a fast path; other requests enter tier two and its tool loop.

00:03:00.414 --> 00:03:08.898
Chat now loads context on demand, so a file question does not automatically fetch GPS or nearby places.

00:03:08.898 --> 00:03:11.726
Both branches eventually persist their results.

00:03:11.726 --> 00:03:19.739
Journey statuses are Created, Active, Paused, Resumed, Completed and Expired; parking and shop context are separate concepts.

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:24.510
There is no PostgreSQL or MySQL server in this checkout.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:28.326
SQLite holds device data and backend local state.

00:03:28.326 --> 00:03:34.527
Firestore holds cloud records, context memory, command requests, notifications, budgets and indexing metadata.

00:03:34.527 --> 00:03:38.343
Cloud Storage holds recording backups and staged imports.

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:42.160
Chroma stores derived file chunks and their vectors.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:55.039
Firestore does not have one uniform layout: reminders and chats use top level collections with user fields, while context and requests use collections under a user document.

00:03:55.039 --> 00:04:01.718
Some task tools write only to backend SQLite, so their cloud durability needs attention.

00:04:01.718 --> 00:04:06.965
A local table does not automatically imply a durable cloud copy.

00:04:06.965 --> 00:04:12.558
A command starts with a stable request identifier and a Firestore claim.

00:04:12.558 --> 00:04:20.015
Fingerprints reject reuse of an identifier for a different message, and leases prevent competing command execution.

00:04:20.015 --> 00:04:25.142
Cloud Tasks can continue the work when the phone connection ends.

00:04:25.142 --> 00:04:29.803
The device receives progress and proposals through the command stream.

00:04:29.803 --> 00:04:39.591
For Calendar writes, the phone shows a concrete proposal, obtains approval, calls Google using its own authorization, and returns the result.

00:04:39.591 --> 00:04:44.717
ETags and receipts protect against changed events and uncertain repeat writes.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:50.777
Ordinary requested notes and reminders are currently allowlisted without a second approval dialog.

00:04:50.777 --> 00:04:52.641
Older documentation says otherwise.

00:04:52.641 --> 00:04:57.710
A background phone event is persisted before reminder rules are evaluated.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:05.084
Immediate context alerts require fresh matching evidence; the ingestion route uses a five minute freshness window.

00:05:05.084 --> 00:05:10.153
Older uploads can still improve history without triggering an immediate alert.

00:05:10.153 --> 00:05:16.605
A one shot reminder and its notification outbox entry are committed together in Firestore.

00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:23.518
Push delivery or polling brings the alert to the phone, and acknowledgement prevents ordinary duplicates.

00:05:23.518 --> 00:05:28.126
Activity delay reminders use durable timer state and task callbacks.

00:05:28.126 --> 00:05:29.970
Sensor gaps remain gaps.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:36.422
The timeline does not prove continuous exercise, and Android scheduling can delay background work.

00:05:36.422 --> 00:05:39.108
Drive retrieval has its own pipeline.

00:05:39.108 --> 00:05:46.271
The phone downloads or exports an authorized source, registers metadata, and uploads bytes to private staging.

00:05:46.271 --> 00:05:54.329
A leased cloud indexing job reads formats, creates bounded chunks, and asks the separate embedding service for vectors.

00:05:54.329 --> 00:06:01.939
The code pins the model revision and checks that vectors have seven hundred and sixty eight dimensions.

00:06:01.939 --> 00:06:07.311
Chroma stores the derived evidence with owner, account and source version metadata.

00:06:07.311 --> 00:06:10.893
Queries embed the question and retrieve filtered evidence.

00:06:10.893 --> 00:06:15.369
Successful staged sources are deleted; failed ones remain for retry.

00:06:15.369 --> 00:06:18.951
Large imports also have a local bulk workflow.

00:06:18.951 --> 00:06:24.323
Direct multimodal file analysis is another path, not the same as indexing.

00:06:24.323 --> 00:06:26.723
The largest boundary is identity.

00:06:26.723 --> 00:06:36.326
The backend currently returns the same local user for every caller; the mobile user header is not authenticated account identity.

00:06:36.326 --> 00:06:44.008
File capabilities and Calendar sessions protect specific paths, but they do not isolate all API data.

00:06:44.008 --> 00:06:46.408
Synchronization also has important gaps.

00:06:46.408 --> 00:06:51.209
The push route can acknowledge records without confirming Firestore persistence.

00:06:51.209 --> 00:06:59.371
Some cloud readers convert errors into empty lists, while the phone interprets missing remote records as deletions.

00:06:59.371 --> 00:07:11.374
Additional concerns include instance local tasks, full collection scans, concurrent session updates, an unbounded direct index upload path, and different frontend and native backend URLs.

00:07:11.374 --> 00:07:22.522
The project already has useful reliability mechanisms: stable event and request IDs, command leases, transactional notifications, cautious write receipts, and selective model use.

00:07:22.522 --> 00:07:34.640
The AI allowance uses durable reservations in Cloud Run and resets by Indian time, but this does not cap indexing, compute, storage or database costs.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:48.696
Before expanding the system, decide whether it is single user or multi user, choose an authoritative durable store for every entity, and define exactly what a sync acknowledgement guarantees.

00:07:48.696 --> 00:07:52.574
Then test outages, retries, races and permission denial.

00:07:52.574 --> 00:07:58.875
The companion report provides detailed diagrams, source evidence and a prioritized action list.
